From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:20:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] hbbs: refuse controllers below a minimum app version

The apps that are already installed never look for updates, and each of
them sends its version in the punch hole request that starts a connection
through this server. hbbs now answers a request from a version below a
minimum with PunchHoleResponse.other_failure, the text every app version
shows instead of connecting. The check comes right after the key check and
before the peer is looked up: only an app with the server's key gets the
text, it learns nothing about the peer, and the peer never hears of the
attempt. The version is what the app says it is, so this makes honest apps
update and is not an access control.

The rule is a file named with --hjv-min-controller-file
(HJV_MIN_CONTROLLER_FILE); without the option hbbs does and logs what it
did before:

  MIN=1.6.0                      x.y.z, empty for no minimum
  FROM=2026-11-22T15:00:00Z      UTC to the second, empty for at once
  TEXT=... %MIN% ... (version not allowed)

hbbs reads the file again every 10 seconds, so a change needs no restart,
and says each change once: "min-controller 1.6.0 from <time>", or "off".
A file it cannot use changes nothing: the last good rule stays in force
(none at the start, where hbbs still starts) and an error says why, again
every hour while the file stays so, as the next start of hbbs drops that
rule. No file means no minimum. What keeps a mistake from doing harm:

- MIN must be exactly x.y.z without zeros in front, as the release tool,
  the apps and hjv-api write a version: get_version_number reads "1.6" as
  1060.
- hbbs reads only what the release tool writes, so that the tool's
  "hbbs-conf --current" never stops at a file hbbs uses: FROM in the one
  form YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ (years 0001-9999, no other offset, no fraction,
  no leap second), and each key right before its "=" ("MIN =" is an error).
- While MIN names a version, the FROM and TEXT lines must be there and
  every line must end with a line break, so that a file caught half
  written is never taken for a shorter rule ("FROM=" alone means at once).
- TEXT must fit what the installed apps do with it, which cannot be
  changed any more: it has "version not allowed" (without "not allowed"
  1.5.1 asks again on a timer for ever, and apps from 1.6.0 on know the
  refusal by the phrase), does not start with "Failed" or a "_tip" word,
  has no braces, no "RustDesk" and no %NAME% left over, and has a space
  after each URL.
- hbbs's own log lines never quote the file, because TEXT may carry a
  download link that is only for apps with the server's key (at the trace
  level the WebSocket library logs every frame it sends, the refusals
  among them). For the same reason a server that checks no key (-k "")
  runs without a minimum and says so.
- A version is counted like get_version_number, but an input that function
  overflows on (a panic in a debug build) or that is longer than 64 bytes
  counts as 0 and is refused, like an empty version.
- A refusal is logged with the address, the ID and the version, cut to 64
  and 32 characters and quoted; beyond 30 in 10 seconds they are counted
  in one line.

tests/min_version.rs runs hbbs with such files: 1.5.1, 1.5.0, an empty
version, 1.5.1+120 and 1.4.3 are refused and the peer hears nothing,
1.6.0 and later go through; %MIN%; the two texts of the design; no option,
no file, a time still to come, a time reached while running; an edited,
emptied, removed and broken file within 10 seconds; bad files at the
start; a wrong key; plain TCP; and what a controller can put in the log.
The unit tests also read a rule after 64 KiB of comments, take a TEXT of
2000 characters and not one more, say a file that cannot be used again
after an hour of looks, and refuse the times and keys the tool does not
write.

